  "handbook.strait_crossings.title": "Strait crossings",
  "handbook.strait_crossings.body": "Narrow water does not stop an army. Where a real bridge, tunnel or causeway joins two shores, or a strait is narrow enough for a ferry to carry a division across inside a day, the two shores are neighbours: a right click on the far side marches there, supply follows, and a front can run across the water. Gibraltar, the Kerch Strait, the Danish straits, Messina and the four main islands of Japan are all joined this way.\n\n## What it costs\n\n- A bridge, a tunnel or a causeway: the step over it is marched a little slower than open ground.\n- A ferry: the step takes about three times as long, because a division loads, sails and unloads a battalion at a time.\n- Attacking over a crossing is worse than attacking over any river. Formations that come over a bridge keep about three fifths of their strength; those that land from ferries keep less than half. Divisions in the same battle that came by land keep all of theirs.\n\n## Fleets\n\nA ferry does not sail while an enemy fleet commands the strait: enough hostile ships at sea beside it, and clearly more than you and your allies have there. Divisions already ordered across wait on the quay and go on when the water is clear. Bridges, tunnels and causeways stay open; the map marks them amber while a fleet threatens them.\n\n## On the map\n\nCrossings are thin dashed lines between the two shores: pale stone for a fixed link, pale blue for a ferry, red for a ferry an enemy fleet has stopped. The province card names the crossing that starts there and what it costs.\n\n## What a crossing is not\n\nIt is a road, not a border. Two shores joined by a bridge are still separate ground when a peace is drawn, when a region breaks away or when a corridor is bought. Wide water - the Channel, the Taiwan Strait, the Strait of Hormuz - still needs a fleet and a landing."
